WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.250
Eight keystrokes, eight requests. Then 429: Too Many Requests.

2
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:11.280
Nothing is broken. The app is being rate-limited.

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.850
Fixed window: 5 allowed per 10 s, per identity.

4
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:26.620
The sixth gets 429 + Retry-After. Rejected requests are not counted here.

5
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:31.600
Immediate retry: the window hasn't moved. Rejected again.

6
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.200
Observed: 8 keystrokes → 17 requests, 12 × 429.

7
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:44.090
Debounce: send only after 250 ms of no typing.

8
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:48.690
Same 8 keystrokes → 1 request.

9
00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.880
Responses can arrive out of order.

10
00:00:52.880 --> 00:01:01.950
A sequence number: apply a response only if it is still the latest.

11
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.780
AbortController cancels the obsolete request.

12
00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:12.670
An abort does not prove the server stopped processing.

13
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:19.010
Retry-After: round up, wait, retry the current query at most once.

14
00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:25.350
Query changed? Cancel the retry. Real 429s may omit the header.

15
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:31.360
Flawed: 17 requests, 12 × 429, results for "p".

16
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:37.370
Corrected: 1 request, 0 × 429, results for "postgres".

17
00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.720
Coordinate the work. Don't just repeat it faster.

18
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:48.250
Debouncing is not server-side limiting. Don't reuse this retry policy for writes.
